---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Waltz — Igor Khokhriakov"
  description="A browser interface for scientific control systems, built in 2016 so facility teams could operate instruments and extend the UI with their own components. Ten years of development, 50 releases, adopted across Tango Controls facilities."
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="container case-study">

      <header class="cs-header">
        <p class="section-marker">Case study · user-facing platform · since 2016</p>
        <h1>Instrument control in the browser for scientific facilities.</h1>
        <p class="cs-lead">
          Operators, beamline scientists, and control engineers at accelerators, synchrotrons,
          and neutron sources ran their instruments through native desktop clients wired to
          the hardware. Waltz put that work in a browser and let each facility extend it with
          its own instrument components. I designed it, built it, and maintained it for ten
          years.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-links">
          <a href="https://github.com/scientific-software-hub/waltz" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[source]</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/orgs/waltz-controls/packages" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[packages]</a>
          <a href="https://waltz-docs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[docs]</a>
          <a href="https://accelconf.web.cern.ch/icalepcs2019/papers/wesh3003.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[paper: ICALEPCS 2019]</a>
          <a href="https://vimeo.com/268669625" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[video: UI overview 2018]</a>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section class="cs-section" data-reveal>
        <h2>Problem</h2>
        <p>
          A control-room operator needs live values from hundreds of devices, plots, and the
          ability to change a setting safely. The tools for that were desktop clients tied to
          one machine and one installation. Every facility also has instruments nobody else
          has, so each one needs its own panels, and each used to mean a fork or a custom
          application. Installing, updating, and sharing those panels was slow, which made
          experiments and commissioning slower.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section" data-reveal>
        <h2>Discovery</h2>
        <p>
          The need surfaced through hands-on work at DESY and conversations across the Tango
          Controls community, where web access to control systems kept coming up as a gap.
          No formal user research preceded the first version. I built a small working
          interface early, put it in front of operators and developers, and let their
          requests shape the roadmap. The 2019 workshops (below) became the structured
          feedback loop.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section" data-reveal>
        <h2>Decisions</h2>
        <h3>Browser over another desktop client</h3>
        <p>
          A browser removes installation and update friction, works on any operator's machine,
          and lets a facility share one deployment. The cost was that nobody had solved live
          hardware streaming in a browser yet, which led to the libraries below.
        </p>

        <h3>A written contract before the code</h3>
        <p>
          The Tango REST API specification came first, as a formal document, before the server
          code that implemented it. Any conforming server worked with Waltz, and any
          conforming client worked with any conforming server. The community adopted it as the
          standard interface between web clients and control systems.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-proof">
          <a href="https://github.com/tango-controls/rest-api" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[Tango REST spec]</a>
        </div>

        <h3>Extension without forking</h3>
        <p>
          Facilities needed their own instrument widgets, so the plugin contract became the
          product's main design constraint. A facility adds a widget without touching the
          core, and that widget survives the next upstream release.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section" data-reveal>
        <h2>What I shipped</h2>
        <p>
          The application plus three independently published libraries, each solving a problem
          that had no off-the-shelf answer in 2016.
        </p>

        <h3>A reliable Server-Sent Events source</h3>
        <p>
          Operators need live values without polling. The browser's native
          <code>EventSource</code> reconnects but does not recover missed events, dedupe across
          reconnects, or multiplex subscriptions over one connection. This library does all
          three, so the UI can treat a hardware stream as a dependable feed.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-proof">
          <a href="https://github.com/orgs/waltz-controls/packages" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[@waltz-controls/eventbus]</a>
        </div>

        <h3>An event-bus SPA middleware</h3>
        <p>
          Independently developed components publish and subscribe on a central bus instead
          of calling each other. That decoupling made third-party plugins possible, because a
          plugin only speaks the bus protocol and shares nothing else with the host.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-proof">
          <a href="https://github.com/orgs/waltz-controls/packages" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[@waltz-controls/middleware]</a>
        </div>

        <h3>A framework-agnostic plugin model</h3>
        <p>
          Widgets register against the bus at startup and resolve at render time. The
          production build ships Webix widgets, CodeMirror, and Plotly side by side, and the
          same model accommodates React or Angular widgets without changing core code.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-proof">
          <a href="https://github.com/orgs/waltz-controls/packages" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[@waltz-controls/tango-rest-client]</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/waltz-controls" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[GitHub org]</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section" data-reveal>
        <h2>Outcome and learning</h2>
        <p>
          Three workshops at DESY in 2019 brought developers from multiple facilities together
          to build extensions collaboratively. Maintainers elsewhere could diagnose and fix
          issues without me, because the libraries and the plugin contract were independently
          documented and versioned. The Tango REST specification became the community
          standard, and Waltz stayed in active development for ten years.
        </p>
        <p>
          The lasting lesson: the durable parts of a platform are its contracts and libraries,
          and the application is the first customer of them. Looking back, I would invest in
          usage telemetry and structured operator interviews from the first release, which
          would have replaced informal feedback with measured adoption data.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-proof">
          <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rKnVGFP_VCA" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[video: Workshop I]</a>
          <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npj8paRuF0Q" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[video: Workshop II]</a>
          <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2ryBt_Cztb0" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="proof-link">[video: Workshop III]</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section" data-reveal>
        <h2>Numbers</h2>
        <div class="cs-numbers">
          <div class="cs-num">
            <span class="metric cs-num-val">2016</span>
            <span class="cs-num-label">among the first web SCADA UIs</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-num">
            <span class="metric cs-num-val">10</span>
            <span class="cs-num-label">years active development</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-num">
            <span class="metric cs-num-val">50</span>
            <span class="cs-num-label">releases</span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-num">
            <span class="metric cs-num-val">757</span>
            <span class="cs-num-label">commits</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-section" data-reveal>
        <h2>Stack</h2>
        <p class="mono" style="color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem;">
          JavaScript · RxJS · Webix · CodeMirror · Plotly · webpack · Server-Sent Events ·
          REST · Docker · Tomcat · Tango Controls · EPICS · TINE · DOOCS
        </p>
      </section>

      <footer class="cs-footer">
        <p>
          What this demonstrates: building a product for specialist users, choosing the
          boundaries that let other teams extend it, and staying with it long enough to see
          it adopted. The web app was the occasion; the contracts and libraries were the
          lasting work.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
          <a href="/" class="mono">← Back to home</a>
          <span style="margin-inline: 1rem; color: var(--border)">·</span>
          <a href="/trading-platform/" class="mono">Trading platform case study →</a>
        </p>
      </footer>

    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 5rem); }

  .cs-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 3rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .cs-header h1 { margin-block: 1rem 1.25rem; max-width: 24ch; }
  .cs-lead { color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.05rem; margin-top: 0; text-align: justify; }

  .cs-links { display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 1.25rem; }

  .cs-section {
    margin-bottom: 2.75rem;
    padding-bottom: 2.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .cs-section:last-of-type { border-bottom: none; }
  .cs-section h2 { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .cs-section h3 { margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--text); }
  .cs-section p { text-align: justify; }

  .cs-proof { display: flex; gap: 0.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 0.75rem; }

  .cs-numbers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(130px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .cs-num { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.25rem; }
  .cs-num-val {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-stretch: expanded;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .cs-num-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  .cs-footer {
    padding-top: 2.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  .cs-footer p:first-child { text-align: justify; }
</style>
